      <article class="shell stack">
        <!-- HERO -->
        <section class="surface panel">
          <p class="eyebrow">Adult article</p>
          <h1>How to stop overthinking (without suppressing it)</h1>

          <p class="lead">
            Trying to suppress worry can make it feel louder. This guide shows
            how to give recurring worries a specific place, so they do not take
            over every part of your day.
          </p>
<div class="blog-answer"><strong>In short:</strong> Trying to suppress worry can make it feel louder. This guide shows
            how to give recurring worries a specific place, so they do not take
            over every part of your day.</div>

        </section>

        <!-- QUICK ANSWER -->
        <section class="surface panel">
          <h2>The idea in brief</h2>

          <p>
            Scheduled worry time reduces overthinking by postponing worries to a
            fixed daily window. Instead of engaging with every thought
            immediately, you contain them and deal with them later in a
            controlled way.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- PROBLEM -->
        <section class="surface panel">
          <h2>Why worrying feels uncontrollable</h2>

          <p>
            Worry often feels automatic because it gets reinforced throughout
            the day. Every time you engage with a thought, your brain learns
            that it is important and should be revisited.
          </p>

          <p>
            Over time, this creates constant mental background noise — where
            even small triggers lead to extended overthinking.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- REFRAME -->
        <section class="surface panel">
          <h2>A different approach: don’t stop worry — contain it</h2>

          <p>
            Instead of trying to eliminate worry, this method changes when and
            how you engage with it.
          </p>

          <p>
            You set aside a specific time each day where worrying is allowed.
            Outside of that window, you postpone thoughts instead of following
            them.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- METHOD -->
        <section class="surface panel">
          <h2>How the method works</h2>

          <p>
            During the day, when a worry appears, you briefly note it and tell
            yourself you will return to it later. This creates distance without
            suppression.
          </p>

          <p>
            At your scheduled time, you review these thoughts. Some will already
            feel less important. Others can be sorted into:
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Problems you can act on</strong> and
            <strong>uncertainties you cannot control.</strong>
          </p>

          <p>
            You make simple plans for the first, and practice letting go of the
            second.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- WHY IT WORKS -->
        <section class="surface panel">
          <h2>Why this works</h2>

          <p>
            This method breaks the habit of immediate engagement. By postponing
            worry, you teach your brain that thoughts do not require instant
            attention.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also reduces emotional intensity. Many worries lose urgency by
            the time you revisit them.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- PRACTICAL DETAILS -->
        <section class="surface panel">
          <h2>Making it practical</h2>

          <p>
            Choose a fixed 15–30 minute window, preferably earlier in the
            evening. Use the same place each day, and stop when the time ends —
            even if everything is not resolved.
          </p>

          <p>
            Afterward, shift into another activity to signal closure. This
            prevents worry from continuing into the rest of your evening.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- LINK TO SLEEP -->
        <section class="surface-soft panel">
          <h2>Why this also improves sleep</h2>

          <p>
            Many people experience overthinking at night. Containing worry
            earlier in the day reduces mental activation at bedtime.
          </p>

          <p>
            If sleep is also affected, combining this with
            <a class="button-ghost" href="/blog/pages/adult/stimulus-control-therapy"><strong>stimulus control therapy</strong></a>
            can further stabilise the system.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- EXPECTATIONS -->
        <section class="surface panel">
          <h2>What to expect</h2>

          <p>
            At first, postponing worries may feel unnatural or difficult. With
            repetition, the brain gradually adapts, and intrusive thoughts
            become less persistent.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- CTA -->
        <section class="surface panel">
          <h2>Need structured help?</h2>

          <p>
            If overthinking continues to interfere with daily functioning or
            sleep, structured CBT techniques can help you regain control more
            effectively.
          </p>

          <div class="cta-row">
            <a class="button" href="/book">Book a consultation</a>
            <a class="button-ghost" href="/blog">Read more articles</a>
          </div>
        </section>
      </article>
    </main></div>
